/-!
# The dyadic rationals `ℤ[1/2]` and the literal affine group `BS(1,2)`

The manuscript's cyclic comparison --- `non_mf_groups_exist.tex`,
`\section{The cyclic comparison}` (`\ref{sec:necessity}`), Theorem
`\ref{thm:cyclic}`, its proof, and the paragraph
`\paragraph{Sharpness of the Kazhdan hypothesis.}` --- realizes the marked
word inside the coset action of the *affine* group `BS(1,2) ≅ ℤ[1/2] ⋊ ℤ`.
(`thm:C` is a legacy alias for `thm:cyclic` and is no longer a `\label`; the
manuscript's own note beside the theorem says so.  Navigate by label, never by
line: the `.tex` is edited concurrently and every offset drifts.)
The sentence this file discharges is
`The affine group $\mathrm{BS}(1,2)\cong\mathbb Z[1/2]\rtimes\mathbb Z$ is
solvable.`: the isomorphism is *asserted* there, so it is proved here rather
than assumed.
This file builds that literal group and proves the isomorphism the manuscript
asserts.  Nothing here is an overgroup surrogate: the base is exactly the ring
`ℤ[1/2]`, and `Affine` is exactly `ℤ[1/2] ⋊ ℤ` with `ℤ` acting by doubling.

## Contents

* `dyadicSubring : Subring ℚ` --- the dyadic rationals, together with
  `dyadicSubring_eq_closure`, which identifies it with the subring of `ℚ`
  generated by `1/2`.  That identification is what licenses the name `ℤ[1/2]`.
* `doubleFun`, `halveFun`, `doublingAut` --- multiplication by `2`, which is an
  *automorphism* of `ℤ[1/2]` (it is only an injective non-surjective
  endomorphism of the subgroup `ℤ`; the dyadic rationals are precisely the
  colimit that repairs this).
* `Affine := Multiplicative ℤ[1/2] ⋊ Multiplicative ℤ`, with the two
  distinguished elements `gammaZero` (translation by `1`) and `tZero` (the
  stable letter), satisfying `conj_gammaZero : t γ₀ t⁻¹ = γ₀²`.
* `hnnWord t a k n = t⁻ⁿ aᵏ tⁿ` in an arbitrary group carrying the compression
  relation, and the resulting universal property `affineLift`: every pair
  `(t, a)` with `t a t⁻¹ = a²` receives a homomorphism from `ℤ[1/2] ⋊ ℤ`.
* `BS`, the presented group `⟨a, t | t a t⁻¹ = a²⟩`, and
  `bsEquivAffine : BS ≃* Affine`, the manuscript's identification.

The universal property is exactly the normal-form content of the ascending HNN
extension: `affineLift` is built by hand from the words `t⁻ⁿ aᵏ tⁿ`, so the
isomorphism is proved, not assumed.
-/

set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

namespace GroupApproximation
